</details>

**Overly broad cleanup deletes all customers indiscriminately.**

The `tearDown()` method calls `Customer::get_all()` which retrieves all customers in the database via the inherited Base_Model method that queries without filtering, then deletes every customer found. This violates test isolation—data created by other tests or test runs could be deleted, risking parallel test failures.

Additionally, only one test in this file explicitly calls `save()` (line 325), making the database cleanup largely unnecessary since `WP_UnitTestCase::tearDown()` typically resets database state between tests.

**Recommended approach:**

Track created customers in an instance property and delete only those:

```diff
 class Customer_Test extends WP_UnitTestCase {
+
+	/**
+	 * Customers created during tests.
+	 *
+	 * @var Customer[]
+	 */
+	private $created_customers = [];
+
+	/**
+	 * Helper to create and track a customer.
+	 */
+	private function create_and_track_customer(): Customer {
+		$customer = new Customer();
+		$this->created_customers[] = $customer;
+		return $customer;
+	}

	/**
	 * Tear down test environment.
	 */
	public function tearDown(): void {
-		// Clean up created customers
-		$customers = Customer::get_all();
-		if ($customers) {
-			foreach ($customers as $customer) {
-				if ($customer->get_id()) {
-					$customer->delete();
-				}
+		// Clean up only customers created by this test
+		foreach ($this->created_customers as $customer) {
+			if ($customer->get_id()) {
+				$customer->delete();
			}
		}
-		
+		$this->created_customers = [];
+
		parent::tearDown();
	}
```

Update `test_verification_key_functionality` (and any future tests that persist) to use `$this->create_and_track_customer()`.



