body:
_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Missing authorization check in credentials modal.**

The `render_credentials_modal` method retrieves and displays sensitive email account credentials (including passwords) without verifying that the current user owns the account. Unlike `handle_create_email_form` and `handle_delete_email_form`, there's no ownership verification.


<details>
<summary>🔎 Proposed fix to add authorization check</summary>

```diff
 public function render_credentials_modal(): void {

 	$email_account_id = wu_request('email_account_id');
 	$email_account    = wu_get_email_account($email_account_id);

 	if ( ! $email_account) {
 		echo '<p>' . esc_html__('Email account not found.', 'ultimate-multisite') . '</p>';
 		return;
 	}

+	// Verify ownership
+	$current_user_id = get_current_user_id();
+	$customer        = $email_account->get_customer();
+
+	if ( ! is_super_admin() && ( ! $customer || $customer->get_user_id() !== $current_user_id)) {
+		echo '<p>' . esc_html__('You do not have permission to view these credentials.', 'ultimate-multisite') . '</p>';
+		return;
+	}
+
 	// Get password from token if available
```
</details>

<details>
<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>

```
In inc/ui/class-email-accounts-element.php around lines 449 to 470, the
credentials modal renders sensitive account data without verifying the current
user owns or is permitted to view the account; add an authorization check before
accessing password/token: get the current user ID (get_current_user_id()),
compare it to the email account owner ID (use whatever property or meta the
codebase uses for ownership), and if the user is not the owner and does not have
an appropriate capability (e.g., current_user_can('manage_options') or a
site-specific capability), echo an error message and return so the
password/token is never exposed to unauthorized users.
```

</details>

